{
  "dom_id": "step-e21645",
  "name": "Example: Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues",
  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system.",
  "worked_solution": "The matrix of our system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/7d16f04f098fc32370423b1b7adaa84c14b92291bde82672b65977b0498ec011.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/32f9fd5ff34d75a7b82427a5ab7233613da3bdbaefdaef37058e65b1691b6c4a.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
